# Line endings for the shipped tree.
#
# Why this file exists, since it is easy to delete as noise: the guards in
# `.claude/hooks/` and the spec engine in `scripts/spec/` are bash scripts invoked
# by a shebang. A repo whose own .gitattributes sets `* text=auto eol=crlf` - a real
# and common setting on Windows-first teams, shipped by real repositories - checks
# these out with CRLF endings. The kernel then reads `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` and
# fails to find an interpreter named `bash\r`, so the script exits 127 with empty
# stdout.
#
# For a hook whose entire job is to DENY a dangerous command, that failure mode is
# the dangerous one: it does not deny and it does not complain. It says nothing, and
# the command proceeds. Verified against a real repo shipping exactly that setting -
# all ten shipped .sh files exited 127 with no output against a destructive command.
#
# So the line endings of these files are not a preference, they are a correctness
# property, and this file is what holds it when the surrounding repo disagrees.

*.sh text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf

# Files without an extension that are still scripts - hooks land here.
.claude/hooks/* text eol=lf
scripts/spec/* text eol=lf
